ion of Mesopotamia, which was the heart of the Akkadian Empire."}], "places": [{"name": "Akkad, Iraq", "role": "Cultural center"}, {"name": "Lullubi, Iraq", "role": "Military campaign site"}, {"name": "Nippur, Iraq", "role": "Religious significance"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Stele of Naram-Sim", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "stele-of-naram-sim"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q897474", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victory_Stele_of_Naram-Sin"], "tags": ["archaeological artefact", "Sculpture & Monuments", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Stele of Naram-Sim contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Stele of Naram-Sin symbolizes the rise of imperial power in Mesopotamia, influencing subsequent rulers and the use of propaganda in art, ultimately shaping the political landscape of the region.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-08T17:09:52Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28960855275", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Old-akkadian victory stele Created by http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/9afafa1f965ece1b8b0b9b94647c7499. Dating to c. 2350 BCE.", "newValue": "The Stele of Naram-Sin, crafted circa 2350 BCE, stands as a monumental testament to the Akkadian Empire and its formidable leader, Naram-Sin, who is depicted triumphantly ascending a mountain after a victorious campaign against the Lullubi people. This artifact, located in modern-day Iraq, serves not only as a representation of military prowess but also as an early example of the use of art for political propaganda, illustrating the transition from city-states to empires in the ancient Near East. The stele symbolizes a key moment in history when the Akkadian culture began to assert its influence over a vast territory, marking a pivotal shift in governance and societal organization.

The stele's narrative is rich with symbolism; it showcases Naram-Sin adorned with a horned helmet, a divine attribute that suggests his semi-divine status, thereby legitimizing his rule. The accompanying cuneiform inscription boldly declares his victories, effectively serving as both a record of military achievements and a means to convey the divine favor he believed he possessed. This blending of religious and political imagery would set a precedent for future rulers in the region, who would similarly seek to intertwine their legitimacy with divine endorsement.

